: : 	Luckily, it's really easy.  What you need to do is set the
: : executable as SUID.  The command should be something like "chmod u+s
: : nethack" depending on your system. This causes all files accessed or generated
: : by the program to think that the user is you.  Of course, the executable
: : and its directory must be publicly readable.
: 
: I have installed Nethack in my user directory on our local system.  Can I use
: the same method?  Using this method it is just the nethack executable and
: root directory that need world read access?  All files/directories below
: them simply require the standard user read/write/execute?
: 
Yup.  Nethack is running in my user directory on our system as SUID.  All I
have needed to do is make the executable and its directory world-readable.
I have often heard that it is somewhat a better idea to make the game SGID
"games" instead, if you can (chmod g+s nethack).  I think the S*ID stuff is
pretty secure if handled properly, but be forewarned that it still might
pose a security hazard.

